    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00004">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <para id="para-00068">For numbers 1-2, use the equations below:</para>
        <quote id="quote-00002">These are the equations from the activity. Each equation is in the
          form <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>A</m:mi>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mi>B</m:mi>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>C</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math><newline/>
        <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math><newline/>
          <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>12</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>8</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math><newline/>
          <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>8</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>16</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math><newline/>
          <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math><newline/>
          <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>4</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>6</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>
          </quote>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00071">Observe the graph of the equation <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> where it is
              graphed, on the same coordinate plane, with another line passing through <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>A</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>B</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.
              In this example <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>A</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>B</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>, so the other line
              would pass through <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>−</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.
              What do you notice about the lines?</para>
                      <media alt="insert alt text">
          <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
        </media>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00072">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00073">Answers may vary, but here is a sample. The two lines are
              perpendicular.</para>
            <para id="para-00074">

              <media alt="Graph with two intersecting lines. One line passes through points (0, 0) and (-4, 3); the other, steeper line Intersects the first line. Both axes are labeled, and gridlines are visible.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_13_06_02.jpg"/>
              </media>

            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00075">Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the
